From 6d25cdb4ac2d5d3f2a13adf387b3ddf630b935a2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sbihaiko <bihaiko@protonmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 08:36:42 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] texture_cache: delete images whose memory went entirely
 to newer images, with EDEN_NX2_TC_SUPERSEDED=1

Ocarina of Time moves its render targets within a memory pool every frame
while the camera moves (about 45 images a frame in the Castle Town guard
house). The old images stay registered until the collector reaches them,
far slower than they arrive, and VRAM spills.

At the frame boundary, delete a bad-overlapping image unused for 2 frames
once the images prepared after it cover all of its range, without a download
when nothing is lost: the image holds no GPU data, or every covering image
is GPU-written and cleared its whole surface after the image's last write
(full_write_tick), so a guest read or a join sees the newer bytes either
way. Covers that were only drawn into, partial covers, aliased, sparse and
unbacked images are left to the collector. Switch 2 titles only. The pages
are not marked stale: the covering images hold the bytes, and a stale page
lets an image made over them skip its upload without being filled.

The decision is a pure function (superseded_images.h). The 600-frame
statistics block gets a line for the deletions and the GPU-written
candidates kept for lack of proof.

Differences from Lichii: this core lacks #817 (skip the upload of a new
target a clear overwrites whole), so ClearCovers and ClearOverwritesWhole
are added as new functions; IsFullClear and JoinImages are unchanged.
PartialDownload images (pending partial readback, GpuModified may be clear)
are excluded as candidates and as covers. last_use_frame and
full_write_tick are kept only with the switch on.

Off by default; with the switch unset nothing is scanned or deleted. Port
of Lichii #1314 (commit 2 of 3); its Catch2 test is run as a host test
outside the tree, with one more case for PartialDownload.
